msgid "Use the Python console"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Run Python on the open study, with every change one step of undo and its"
" code recorded.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Python console** runs Python on the study open in EMStudio, through"
" the ``em`` library of s3Dgraphy, the same that EM Tools and the plugins "
"use. It reads the graph, the selection and the vocabularies, and it "
"writes through ``em.write``: each write is one step of ``Edit ▸ Undo``, a"
" line in the Log, and an act whose chain keeps the code that ran. The "
"console is marked *beta*, a young function, and a Python script has the "
"powers of your user on this computer."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

msgid "Use the desktop application: the console works through its bridge."
msgstr ""

msgid "Open a study. The console works on the open one; with none, it says so."
msgstr ""

msgid "Open the console"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Choose ``Tools ▸ Python console… · beta``. A window **Python Console** "
"opens under the active window, or the one this workspace already has "
"comes forward. It is a type of window like the others: any area of any "
"workspace can become a console from the window type menu of its header, "
"and the **Scripting** workspace has one ready, under the **Script "
"Editor**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Its head says **Python · em on «…»** with the name of the open study, and"
" **beta**. The first line reminds you that ``em`` is imported, that ``g``"
" is ``em.graph``, the graph shown, and that ``sel`` is the selection."
msgstr ""

msgid "Read the study"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Python console after the line sel: the identifier of the selected "
"unit printed under it."
msgstr ""

msgid "Select SU001 in the Matrix."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the field at the bottom, write ``sel`` and press :kbd:`Enter`. The "
"console prints, under your line, the list of the selected nodes (their "
"identifiers): ``sel`` is read again at every run."
msgstr ""

msgid "Press **Help** to print ``em.help()``: what ``em`` offers here."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":kbd:`Enter` runs a complete line; for a block of several lines, end it "
"with an empty line, or press :kbd:`⌘Enter` (:kbd:`Ctrl+Enter`) to run at "
"once. :kbd:`Shift+Enter` starts a new line. :kbd:`Tab` completes a name."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The head of the window also has the link **Python API reference** (the "
"same entry is in the ``Help`` menu): it opens the complete reference of "
"``em``, the `EMStudio Python API "
"<https://docs.extendedmatrix.org/projects/emstudio-python-"
"api/en/latest/>`__, in the browser on the desktop and in a new tab on the"
" web."
msgstr ""

msgid "Write into the study"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A write is a block opened by ``em.write`` with a label. The label names "
"the step in the Log and in ``Edit ▸ Undo``."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Before writing, press **Try** (:kbd:`⌘⇧Enter`, :kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+Enter`). "
"The same code runs with every write made a proof: the console shows, line"
" by line, what it would add or change, and that nothing was applied."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Run**. The console says **written** with the label, the number of"
" nodes, and that its code is in the chain of the act. The **Inspector** "
"shows the new description at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "``Edit ▸ Undo`` takes the whole block back as one step."
msgstr ""

msgid "The history"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":kbd:`↑` and :kbd:`↓` bring back the code you ran, also from earlier "
"sessions: the history belongs to this computer. :kbd:`Ctrl+R` searches "
"it; :kbd:`Enter` takes the line found and :kbd:`Esc` goes back. **Clear**"
" (:kbd:`Ctrl+L`) empties the console's lines and keeps the history."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Python on the study, with the safety of the rest of EMStudio: every "
"change one step of undo, a line in the Log, and an act that records the "
"code that made it, so that a script's work can be read and checked like "
"any other."
msgstr ""

msgid "Write a script"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The **Script Editor** is the other window of Python, beside the console "
"in the **Scripting** workspace (see :doc:`/reference/workspaces`). It "
"keeps a script in a ``.py`` file of the ``scripts/`` folder beside the "
"``em.json``; a study with no folder keeps its scripts in the browser."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**New**, **Open**, **Save** and **Save as…** in its header work on the "
"scripts of the project; a ● in the title marks a script with unsaved "
"changes."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**▶ Run Script** (:kbd:`Alt+P`, as in Blender) runs the script in the "
"console's session: what it prints goes to the console."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Try** (:kbd:`Alt+Shift+P`) runs the same script with every ``em.write``"
" as a proof: what it would write, nothing applied."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Templates** offers examples from the Python API reference to start from."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`install-packages-and-plugins`, for the plugins, and the development"
" folder for a plugin you are writing."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`/how-to/sources/declare-provenance`, to read the chain of an act."
msgstr ""

